00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:37.633
The microcosmos is home to many unusual partnerships.

00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:42.969
There are species of algae buried inside of
paramecium, exchanging their photosynthetic

00:00:42.969 --> 00:00:45.258
products for protection.

00:00:45.258 --> 00:00:51.938
There are cyanobacteria fixing nitrogen within
the glass walls of their diatom host.

00:00:51.938 --> 00:01:00.379
Life is, after all, just relationships, individual
entities fighting and tolerating and partnering

00:01:00.379 --> 00:01:06.710
in tiny little dramas, each of which build
upon one another like strokes of paint in

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:11.067
an epic tableau of ecology, epidemics,

00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:12.662
and yogurt?

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.820
Yes, yogurt.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:26.030
That plastic tub tucked away in the dairy
section of your local grocery store is the

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:31.770
product of one of the most fruitful microbial
friendships that humans have unknowingly played

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:36.402
matchmaker to, a friendship that goes back
millennia.

00:01:36.402 --> 00:01:41.880
That relationship is playing out in front
of your eyes right now, extracted from some

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:46.543
yogurt that James, our master of microscopes,
had on hand.

00:01:46.543 --> 00:01:52.950
We don’t know how exactly yogurt was invented,
just that it is an ancient food that takes

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:56.019
on many names in different cultures.

00:01:58.268 --> 00:02:05.530
Humans began consuming dairy somewhere between
10,000-5,000 BCE, likely coinciding with our

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:10.601
domestication of animals like cows and goats
that could provide us with milk.

00:02:10.601 --> 00:02:15.124
And the challenge those humans faced is one
many of us can relate to:

00:02:15.124 --> 00:02:19.862
if you leave milk out, it goes bad quite easily.

00:02:19.862 --> 00:02:25.000
In the Middle East and Western Asia, nomadic
herdsmen would carry milk in bags made from

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:30.250
animals that retained some of their enzymes,
which may have then fermented the milk and

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:31.767
preserved it.

00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:36.800
And over time, yogurt spread through different
cultures not just because it was effective

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:42.378
as a milk storage method, but also because
it seemed to be quite good for the stomach.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:51.190
According to one story, King Francois I of
France was suffering from severe diarrhea.

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:56.750
To help him out, his Turkish ally Suleiman
the Magnificentt suggested yogurt, bringing

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:59.011
yogurt to the rest of Europe.

00:02:59.011 --> 00:03:01.932
At least, that’s one of the stories.

00:03:01.932 --> 00:03:08.581
Yogurt has a surprisingly mythic aspect to
it, thanks to its longevity, popularity, and

00:03:08.581 --> 00:03:10.540
cryptic origins.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:16.740
And in the early 20th century, one Bulgarian
scientist sought to untangle some of the mysteries

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.185
behind yogurt.

00:03:18.185 --> 00:03:25.480
His name was Stamen Grigorov, and in the year
1904 he was headed to the Medical University

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:28.061
of Geneva to study medicine.

00:03:28.061 --> 00:03:32.477
And he brought with him a clay pot containing
homemade yogurt.

00:03:32.477 --> 00:03:37.360
Grigorov’s work came about fifty years after
Louis Pasteur pieced together the role of

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.383
yeast in the fermentation of alcohol.

00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:45.148
But the organisms behind yogurt’s genius
were still unknown.

00:03:45.148 --> 00:03:51.620
And as a Bulgarian stocked with both his homeland’s
yogurt and curiosity, Grigorov decided it

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.549
was time to take a closer look.

00:03:54.549 --> 00:03:56.989
He found two bacteria.

00:03:56.989 --> 00:04:03.824
The first was shaped like a rod, and it would be 
named Bacillus bulgaricus in honor of its origins.

00:04:03.824 --> 00:04:09.480
With time, the species’ name would be changed,
first to Lactobacillus bulgaricus, and then

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:14.209
to Lactobacillus delbrueckii subsp. bulgaricus.

00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:18.729
Because that’s quite a lot to say, we’re
going to stick with the slightly incorrect

00:04:18.729 --> 00:04:22.627
and older name: Lactobacillus bulgaricus.

00:04:22.627 --> 00:04:28.600
The other bacterium was rounder in shape,
and it would later come to be known as Streptococcus

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:35.540
salivarius subspecies thermophilus, though
we’re going to call it Streptococcus thermophilus.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:41.442
Lactobacillus bulgaricus and Streptococcus
thermophilus may look different from one another.

00:04:41.442 --> 00:04:46.900
But what they share in common is that they
thrive on the lactose sugars present in milk,

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:51.717
digesting them and releasing lactic acid back
into their surroundings.

00:04:51.717 --> 00:04:57.669
As the lactic acid seeps into the milk, the
milk itself becomes less hospitable to other

00:04:57.669 --> 00:05:01.748
microbes, allowing the lactic acid bacteria
to take over.

00:05:01.748 --> 00:05:06.020
But if you’ve ever added lemon juice to
milk, you probably are familiar with the other

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:08.321
effect the lactic acid has:

00:05:08.321 --> 00:05:12.735
it curdles the milk, the result of proteins
breaking apart.

00:05:12.735 --> 00:05:19.100
But Lactobacillus bulgaricus and Streptococcus
thermophilus are not simply two bacteria who

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:22.153
happen to co-exist within the same yogurt.

00:05:22.153 --> 00:05:24.630
These two organisms work together.

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.487
They could survive just fine on their own.

00:05:27.487 --> 00:05:34.183
But when cultured together, they grow faster
thanks to a molecular exchange between the two.

00:05:34.183 --> 00:05:39.870
Lactobacillus bulgaricus offers amino acids
made by breaking down the proteins in milk

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:45.798
to Streptococcus thermophilus, which doesn’t
have the tools it needs to be able to do the same.

00:05:45.798 --> 00:05:51.500
And in return, Streptococcus thermophilus
produces various fatty acids and other chemicals

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.716
that help Lactobacillus bulgaricus grow.

00:05:54.716 --> 00:05:59.356
We don’t know how these two organisms originally
found each other.

00:05:59.356 --> 00:06:04.180
We’re not even entirely sure how they found
their way into milk.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:09.479
Our best theory is that they were both found
on the roots of plants that were boiled with

00:06:09.479 --> 00:06:13.561
sheep’s milk by Bulgarian shepherds who
were making yogurt.

00:06:13.561 --> 00:06:17.740
And their existence has inspired other scientists
as well.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:23.470
Shortly after Grigorov revealed the presence
of Lactobacillus bulgaricus in yogurt, an

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:28.714
immunologist named Elie Metchnikoff became
interested as well.

00:06:28.714 --> 00:06:35.025
Metchnikoff’s work in immunology was influential
and would go on to win him the Nobel prize.

00:06:35.025 --> 00:06:41.389
But he was also fascinated with aging, and
particularly the idea that microbes living

00:06:41.389 --> 00:06:44.676
within us would help us live longer,

00:06:44.676 --> 00:06:49.729
microbe’s like Grigorov’s recently 
discovered Lactobacillus bulgaricus.

00:06:49.729 --> 00:06:53.678
In his book The Prolongation of Life: 
Optimistic Studies,

00:06:53.678 --> 00:06:57.487
Metchnikoff described his 
daily probiotic regiment.

00:06:57.487 --> 00:07:02.300
He would either consume a pure culture of
the bacteria followed by milk, or he would

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:05.707
go for the simpler option: yogurt.

00:07:05.707 --> 00:07:11.810
Metchnikoff didn’t specify exactly how he
measured his satisfaction with his experiment,

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:14.761
just that he was happy with the results.

00:07:14.761 --> 00:07:20.000
But the pathways he thought Lactobacillus
bulgaricus would follow in the body don’t

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:25.340
seem to have been borne out in later experiments,
making his specific theories about their use

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:29.545
as tools to help us live longer unlikely.

00:07:29.545 --> 00:07:35.000
Of course, that is not to say that yogurt
isn’t good for you, just that its benefits

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.047
are different from what Metchnikoff envisioned.

00:07:38.047 --> 00:07:43.840
And moreover, his work got scientists thinking
about using microbes as a sort of medicine

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.068
decades before the term “probiotics” was
even created.

00:07:48.068 --> 00:07:54.259
And it is fitting that Lactobacillus bulgaricus
would catalyze this way of thinking, a microbe

00:07:54.259 --> 00:07:58.917
in partnership with Streptococcus thermophilus
for thousands of years—

00:07:58.917 --> 00:08:02.090
and really, in partnership with us.

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:08.180
Even now, separated from the plants they once
lived on, these bacteria cultivate our yogurt

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:11.506
and feed us, just as they feed each other.

